#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:1
msgid ""
"safer-buffer [![travis][travis-image]][travis-url] [![npm][npm-image"
"]][npm-url] [![javascript style guide][standard-image]][standard-url] "
"[![Security Responsible Disclosure][secuirty-image]][secuirty-url]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:1
msgid "travis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:1
msgid "npm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:1
msgid "javascript style guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:1
msgid "Security Responsible Disclosure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:12
msgid ""
"Modern Buffer API polyfill without footguns, working on Node.js from 0.8 "
"to current."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:14
msgid "How to use?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:16
msgid ""
"First, port all `Buffer()` and `new Buffer()` calls to `Buffer.alloc()` "
"and `Buffer.from()` API."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:18
msgid ""
"Then, to achieve compatibility with outdated Node.js versions (`<4.5.0` "
"and 5.x `<5.9.0`), use `const Buffer = require('safer-buffer').Buffer` in"
" all files where you make calls to the new Buffer API. _Use `var` instead"
" of `const` if you need that for your Node.js version range support._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:22
msgid ""
"Also, see the [porting Buffer](https://github.com/ChALkeR/safer-"
"buffer/blob/master/Porting-Buffer.md) guide."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:25
msgid "Do I need it?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:27
msgid ""
"Hopefully, not — dropping support for outdated Node.js versions should be"
" fine nowdays, and that is the recommended path forward. You _do_ need to"
" port to the `Buffer.alloc()` and `Buffer.from()` though."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:31
msgid ""
"See the [porting guide](https://github.com/ChALkeR/safer-"
"buffer/blob/master/Porting-Buffer.md) for a better description."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:34
msgid "Why not [safe-buffer](https://npmjs.com/safe-buffer)?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:36
msgid ""
"_In short: while `safe-buffer` serves as a polyfill for the new API, it "
"allows old API usage and itself contains footguns._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:39
msgid ""
"`safe-buffer` could be used safely to get the new API while still keeping"
" support for older Node.js versions (like this module), but while "
"analyzing ecosystem usage of the old Buffer API I found out that `safe-"
"buffer` is itself causing problems in some cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:43
msgid "For example, consider the following snippet:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:55
msgid ""
"This is allocates and writes to console an uninitialized chunk of memory."
" [standard](https://www.npmjs.com/package/standard) linter (among others)"
" catch that and warn people to avoid using unsafe API."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:59
msgid "Let's now throw in `safe-buffer`!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:70
msgid ""
"See the problem? Adding in `safe-buffer` _magically removes the lint "
"warning_, but the behavior remains identiсal to what we had before, and "
"when launched on Node.js 6.x LTS — this dumps out chunks of uninitialized"
" memory. _And this code will still emit runtime warnings on Node.js 10.x "
"and above._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:75
msgid ""
"That was done by design. I first considered changing `safe-buffer`, "
"prohibiting old API usage or emitting warnings on it, but that "
"significantly diverges from `safe-buffer` design. After some discussion, "
"it was decided to move my approach into a separate package, and _this is "
"that separate package_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:80
msgid ""
"This footgun is not imaginary — I observed top-downloaded packages doing "
"that kind of thing, «fixing» the lint warning by blindly including `safe-"
"buffer` without any actual changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:83
msgid ""
"Also in some cases, even if the API _was_ migrated to use of safe Buffer "
"API — a random pull request can bring unsafe Buffer API usage back to the"
" codebase by adding new calls — and that could go unnoticed even if you "
"have a linter prohibiting that (becase of the reason stated above), and "
"even pass CI. _I also observed that being done in popular packages._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:88
msgid "Some examples:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:89
msgid ""
"[webdriverio](https://github.com/webdriverio/webdriverio/commit/05cbd3167c12e4930f09ef7cf93b127ba4effae4"
"#diff-124380949022817b90b622871837d56cR31) (a module with 548 759 "
"downloads/month),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:91
msgid ""
"[websocket-stream](https://github.com/maxogden/websocket-"
"stream/commit/c9312bd24d08271687d76da0fe3c83493871cf61) (218 288 d/m, fix"
" in [maxogden/websocket-stream#142](https://github.com/maxogden"
"/websocket-stream/pull/142)),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:93
msgid ""
"[node-serialport](https://github.com/node-serialport/node-"
"serialport/commit/e8d9d2b16c664224920ce1c895199b1ce2def48c) (113 138 d/m,"
" fix in [node-serialport/node-serialport#1510](https://github.com/node-"
"serialport/node-serialport/pull/1510)),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:95
msgid ""
"[karma](https://github.com/karma-"
"runner/karma/commit/3d94b8cf18c695104ca195334dc75ff054c74eec) (3 973 193 "
"d/m, fix in [karma-runner/karma#2947](https://github.com/karma-"
"runner/karma/pull/2947)),"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:97
msgid ""
"[spdy-transport](https://github.com/spdy-http2/spdy-"
"transport/commit/5375ac33f4a62a4f65bcfc2827447d42a5dbe8b1) (5 970 727 "
"d/m, fix in [spdy-http2/spdy-transport#53](https://github.com/spdy-http2"
"/spdy-transport/pull/53))."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:99
msgid "And there are a lot more over the ecosystem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:101
msgid ""
"I filed a PR at [mysticatea/eslint-plugin-"
"node#110](https://github.com/mysticatea/eslint-plugin-node/pull/110) to "
"partially fix that (for cases when that lint rule is used), but it is a "
"semver-major change for linter rules and presets, so it would take "
"significant time for that to reach actual setups. _It also hasn't been "
"released yet (2018-03-20)._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:107
msgid ""
"Also, `safer-buffer` discourages the usage of `.allocUnsafe()`, which is "
"often done by a mistake. It still supports it with an explicit concern "
"barier, by placing it under `require('safer-buffer/dangereous')`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:111
msgid "But isn't throwing bad?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:113
msgid ""
"Not really. It's an error that could be noticed and fixed early, instead "
"of causing havoc later like unguarded `new Buffer()` calls that end up "
"receiving user input can do."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:116
msgid ""
"This package affects only the files where `var Buffer = require('safer-"
"buffer').Buffer` was done, so it is really simple to keep track of things"
" and make sure that you don't mix old API usage with that. Also, CI "
"should hint anything that you might have missed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:120
msgid ""
"New commits, if tested, won't land new usage of unsafe Buffer API this "
"way. _Node.js 10.x also deals with that by printing a runtime depecation "
"warning._"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:123
msgid "Would it affect third-party modules?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:125
msgid ""
"No, unless you explicitly do an awful thing like monkey-patching or "
"overriding the built-in `Buffer`. Don't do that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:128
msgid "But I don't want throwing…"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:130
msgid "That is also fine!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:132
msgid ""
"Also, it could be better in some cases when you don't comprehensive "
"enough test coverage."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:134
msgid ""
"In that case — just don't override `Buffer` and use `var SaferBuffer = "
"require('safer-buffer').Buffer` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:137
msgid ""
"That way, everything using `Buffer` natively would still work, but there "
"would be two drawbacks:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:139
msgid ""
"`Buffer.from`/`Buffer.alloc` won't be polyfilled — use `SaferBuffer.from`"
" and `SaferBuffer.alloc` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:141
msgid ""
"You are still open to accidentally using the insecure deprecated API — "
"use a linter to catch that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:143
msgid ""
"Note that using a linter to catch accidential `Buffer` constructor usage "
"in this case is strongly recommended. `Buffer` is not overriden in this "
"usecase, so linters won't get confused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:146
msgid "«Without footguns»?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:148
msgid ""
"Well, it is still possible to do _some_ things with `Buffer` API, e.g. "
"accessing `.buffer` property on older versions and duping things from "
"there. You shouldn't do that in your code, probabably."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:151
msgid ""
"The intention is to remove the most significant footguns that affect lots"
" of packages in the ecosystem, and to do it in the proper way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/safer-buffer_Readme.md:154
msgid ""
"Also, this package doesn't protect against security issues affecting some"
" Node.js versions, so for usage in your own production code, it is still "
"recommended to update to a Node.js version [supported by "
"upstream](https://github.com/nodejs/release#release-schedule)."
msgstr ""

